 ## Rolling back
 
-Redeploy the previous commit's build. There is no database to migrate back —
-the post catalog and everything else the app reads live in the build itself —
-so a rollback here is just a redeploy.
+There is no database to migrate back — the post catalog and everything else
+the app reads live in the build itself — so a rollback is just getting
+Production to build and serve an older commit again. There is no separate
+rollback pipeline: Production redeploys on every push to `main` (see the
+table above), so rolling back means putting `main` back at the last-known-good
+commit and pushing.
+
+1. **Revert.** `git revert` the commit(s) that shipped the bad change and push
+   to `main`. If the revert doesn't apply cleanly, reset `main` to the
+   last-known-good commit and force-push instead — but say so out loud first
+   (force-pushing `main` rewrites history for everyone else who has it
+   checked out).
+2. **Confirm the new build is actually live.** A push starts a new deploy; it
+   doesn't finish one instantly. Check whatever tells you the Production
+   deploy for that push has completed, then load the production URL and look
+   for the specific thing the bad change touched (a post's title or body, the
+   bio copy, whatever it was) and confirm it's back to the old version. If it
+   still shows the bad version, the deploy hasn't finished yet or didn't pick
+   up the revert — don't move on to step 3 until it has.
+3. **Check the things a bad build tends to break.** The home page loads with
+   the portrait and post list, an individual post page loads, and — since
+   this is the part most likely to be silently wrong — a post with more than
+   one translation still resolves through `shared/resolve-translation.ts` to
+   the right locale. Spot-check a post that has multiple translations and one
+   that has none, not just the one that prompted the rollback.
+4. **Say what happened, in writing, on the revert itself.** Whoever reviews
+   the revert PR is the one person guaranteed to see it, so that review is
+   where the record goes: which commit broke, what it looked like broken, and
+   what "confirmed working again" meant in step 2 and 3. That's what turns
+   this into something the next person can act on without reconstructing it
+   from scratch — including you, at 2am, half-remembering this by morning.
